---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import MetricCard from '../../components/MetricCard.astro';
import RollingMaeChart from '../../components/RollingMaeChart.astro';
import PredictionQualityChart from '../../components/PredictionQualityChart.astro';
import PermutationImportanceChart from '../../components/PermutationImportanceChart.astro';

const systemSteps = [
  'ESPN JSON endpoints',
  'Validation + SQLite',
  'Feature Engineering',
  'Model Training',
  'Evaluation + Reports',
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="NBA Player Stat Prediction — Liam Kinnally"
  description="Technical project page for Liam Kinnally's NBA player-stat prediction pipeline built with Python, scikit-learn, SQLAlchemy, pytest, and GitHub Actions."
>
  <article class="project-page container-shell pt-32 pb-24">
    <a href="/" class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-300 transition hover:text-white">← Back home</a>

    <header class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
      <div class="eyebrow">Machine learning · data engineering · testing</div>
      <h1
        class="scroll-heading-shine gradient-text mt-6 text-5xl font-[780] leading-[1.02] tracking-[-0.05em] sm:text-6xl"
        data-scroll-heading
        data-shine-text="NBA Player Stat Prediction"
      >NBA Player Stat Prediction</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300 sm:text-xl">
        Predicting how many points, assists, and rebounds an NBA player will record in his next game, using only what was known before tipoff. An end-to-end Python pipeline covering data collection, feature engineering, model training, and evaluation against a simple baseline on games the model never saw during training.
      </p>
      <div class="mobile-initial-highlight-group mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        <span class="kbd-tag">Python</span><span class="kbd-tag">scikit-learn</span><span class="kbd-tag">SQLAlchemy</span><span class="kbd-tag">SQLite</span><span class="kbd-tag">Matplotlib</span><span class="kbd-tag">pytest</span><span class="kbd-tag">GitHub Actions</span>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a href="https://github.com/liamkinnally/nba-scoring-model" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="button-primary">View source ↗</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="mt-16 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
      <MetricCard value="1,230" label="Games ingested" />
      <MetricCard value="26,547" label="Player-game rows" />
      <MetricCard value="4.85" label="Points MAE" detail="average miss per prediction" />
      <MetricCard value="23 / 28" label="7-day windows won" detail="points model vs. baseline" />
    </section>

    <section class="mt-24 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[0.72fr_1.28fr]">
      <h2
        class="scroll-heading-shine text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.03em]"
        data-scroll-heading
        data-shine-text="The question"
      >The question</h2>
      <div class="space-y-5 text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300">
        <p>
          How well can you predict an NBA player's next box score (points, assists, rebounds) from only what was known before tipoff? The answer is: not perfectly. In the games set aside for testing, players averaging 19–21 points over their previous ten games went on to score anywhere from 0 to 41. Recent scoring, minutes, rest, and matchup history all carry useful signal, but individual game outcomes remain highly variable.
        </p>
        <p>
          A low error can still be easy to fake if a feature quietly peeks at the game being predicted. So useful performance has to survive an honest test: past information only, games evaluated in order, and a simpler method under the same conditions.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-24">
      <div class="mb-8">
        <div class="eyebrow">System design</div>
        <h2
          class="scroll-heading-shine mt-4 text-3xl font-bold tracking-[-0.035em]"
          data-scroll-heading
          data-shine-text="From source data to reproducible evaluation."
        >From source data to reproducible evaluation.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="scroll-frame-focus scroll-frame-quiet glass-card rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8">
        <div class="grid gap-3 md:grid-cols-5">
          {systemSteps.map((step, i) => (
            <div class="stationary-highlight scroll-frame-focus scroll-frame-quiet relative rounded-xl border border-white/[0.08] bg-white/[0.025] p-4">
              <div class="text-xs font-bold text-blue-200">0{i + 1}</div>
              <div class="mt-3 text-sm font-semibold text-slate-200">{step}</div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-24 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[0.72fr_1.28fr]">
      <h2
        class="scroll-heading-shine text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.03em]"
        data-scroll-heading
        data-shine-text="Keeping the test honest"
      >Keeping the test honest</h2>
      <div class="space-y-5 text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300 [text-wrap:pretty]">
        <p>
          A model that sees any part of the result it is supposed to predict will look accurate for the wrong reason. That failure is called data leakage, and preventing it is why every historical feature uses a strict date cutoff. Jamal Murray’s 5-game and 10-game scoring averages, recent minutes, rest, and team form can only be derived from information available before the game being predicted. Numbering his appearances in order, a prediction for Game 11 can use Games 1–10 but nothing from Game 11 itself. The database query enforces this by requiring each source game to have an earlier date than the game being predicted. Games he did not play are excluded from the averages rather than counted as zero.
        </p>
        <p>
          Chronology also governs tuning and evaluation. The most recent 20% of game dates are reserved for the final test. On the earlier data, expanding-window cross-validation divides the dates into four consecutive blocks, producing three rounds that train on the past and validate on the next block. Later games never inform earlier predictions.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-24">
      <div class="mb-28 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[0.72fr_1.28fr]">
        <h2
          class="scroll-heading-shine text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.03em]"
          data-scroll-heading
          data-shine-text="Evaluation"
        >Evaluation</h2>
        <div class="space-y-5 text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300 [text-wrap:pretty]">
          <p>
            A model can look accurate on its own terms and still add nothing if a much simpler rule does the same job. To test whether the added complexity is actually worthwhile, the tuned gradient boosting models are compared head-to-head with a simple 10-game rolling-average baseline, where the player’s average over their previous 10 games is used as the predicted value for that stat in their next game.
          </p>
          <p>
            The final test contains 5,645 player-game rows that were never used in training. Across that chronological holdout, the points model’s mean absolute error is 4.85&nbsp;— a little under five points per prediction on average. Across the 5,612 rows for which both methods produced predictions, the model achieved a 4.841 MAE compared to 4.883 for the 10-game baseline. Assists showed the same small edge, 1.407 against 1.417, while rebounds were effectively tied.
          </p>
          <p>
            The margins are small because a 10-game average is already a strong baseline here. Even so, the model outperformed the baseline in 23 of 28 trailing seven-day windows for points and 22 of 28 for assists.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mx-auto mb-6 w-fit max-w-full text-[0.8rem] leading-5 text-slate-400">The NBA model visualizations across this site are rebuilt natively from the project’s generated reporting data.</p>
      <RollingMaeChart animateHeading interactivePill />
    </section>

    <section class="mt-28">
      <div class="mb-9 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[0.72fr_1.28fr]">
        <div>
          <div class="eyebrow">Prediction quality</div>
          <h2
            class="scroll-heading-shine mt-4 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.03em]"
            data-scroll-heading
            data-shine-text="Useful signal, with a visible ceiling."
          >Useful signal, with a visible ceiling.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-5 text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300">
          <p>
            The chronological holdout contains 5,645 player-game observations. The model tracks typical scoring outcomes reasonably well, but predictions compress toward the middle instead of following extreme performances all the way up.
          </p>
          <p>
            That limitation is especially clear on ceiling games: every one of the 188 holdout performances of 30+ points was underpredicted, with an average underprediction of 13.9 points.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <PredictionQualityChart />
    </section>

    <section class="mt-28">
      <div class="mb-9 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[0.72fr_1.28fr]">
        <div>
          <div class="eyebrow">Model interpretability</div>
          <h2
            class="scroll-heading-shine mt-4 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.03em]"
            data-scroll-heading
            data-shine-text="Recent scoring drives the model."
          >Recent scoring drives the model.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-5 text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300">
          <p>
            Holdout permutation importance measures how much prediction error increases when the values of one feature are randomly rearranged across the holdout data, breaking its relationship with the outcome while leaving the other features unchanged. The player’s 10-game scoring average is by far the strongest signal in the points model, with recent minutes being the strongest secondary signal.
          </p>
          <p>
            As some of the features contain overlapping information, the importance attributed to any one feature can be reduced because other features may still preserve part of the same signal. These values therefore represent how much the model relies on each feature beyond what is already captured by the other features, rather than the total predictive value of the broader signal that feature represents.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <PermutationImportanceChart />
    </section>

    <section class="mt-28 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[0.72fr_1.28fr]">
      <h2
        class="scroll-heading-shine text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.03em]"
        data-scroll-heading
        data-shine-text="Engineering, not just modeling"
      >Engineering, not just modeling</h2>
      <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="stationary-highlight scroll-frame-focus scroll-frame-quiet scroll-frame-leaf glass-card rounded-xl p-5"><h3 class="font-semibold">Reproducible artifacts</h3><p class="mt-2 leading-7 text-slate-300">Serialized model pipelines include evaluation-window metadata so reported metrics can be reproduced later.</p></div>
        <div class="stationary-highlight scroll-frame-focus scroll-frame-quiet scroll-frame-leaf glass-card rounded-xl p-5"><h3 class="font-semibold">Automated testing</h3><p class="mt-2 leading-7 text-slate-300">A pytest suite runs in GitHub Actions CI to catch regressions across the data and modeling pipeline.</p></div>
        <div class="stationary-highlight scroll-frame-focus scroll-frame-quiet scroll-frame-leaf glass-card rounded-xl p-5"><h3 class="font-semibold">CLI workflow</h3><p class="mt-2 leading-7 text-slate-300">Evaluation reports are regenerated from saved models in one command instead of relying on a notebook-only workflow.</p></div>
        <div class="stationary-highlight scroll-frame-focus scroll-frame-quiet scroll-frame-leaf glass-card rounded-xl p-5"><h3 class="font-semibold">Database-backed pipeline</h3><p class="mt-2 leading-7 text-slate-300">SQLAlchemy and SQLite provide a structured persistence layer for game and player-level records.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="scroll-frame-focus scroll-frame-major mt-24 rounded-2xl border border-blue-200/10 bg-blue-200/[0.035] p-7 sm:p-9">
      <div class="eyebrow">Next</div>
      <h2
        class="scroll-heading-shine mt-4 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.03em]"
        data-scroll-heading
        data-shine-text="See the implementation."
      >See the implementation.</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl leading-7 text-slate-300">The repository contains the data pipeline, feature engineering, model training, automated tests, evaluation CLI, and generated reporting workflow.</p>
      <a href="https://github.com/liamkinnally/nba-scoring-model" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="button-primary mt-6">Open GitHub repository ↗</a>
    </section>
  </article>

  <script>
    const headings = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-scroll-heading]');
    const reducedMotion = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

    if (reducedMotion || !('IntersectionObserver' in window)) {
      headings.forEach((heading) => heading.classList.add('is-visible'));
    } else {
      const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
        (entries) => {
          for (const entry of entries) {
            if (!entry.isIntersecting) continue;
            entry.target.classList.add('is-visible');
            observer.unobserve(entry.target);
          }
        },
        {
          threshold: 0.45,
          rootMargin: '0px 0px -8% 0px',
        },
      );

      headings.forEach((heading) => observer.observe(heading));
    }
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
